#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "86054cff-8fae-561d-a57e-31e573a45a91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21585" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(\\ln 2) &= e^{3(\\ln 2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2e^{\\ln 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\ln 2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(12) + 1(4) \\\\ 1(12) + 2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}24 + 4 \\\\ 12 + 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "63995ae4-21fa-5777-85ca-93585258b9f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21586" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$3x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = 3x$\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$-2x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = -2x$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/329339a73bbba31c17772216e1329304.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "e0888bda-120f-52bb-a6f9-c5af9737139f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21588" :question/problem "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/d259112c09bd560643afeb7ef35f3134.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/1e72cfbafb65f970d697dda4103fc713.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/77ad3d8eb22bdba17fcf14e48052927e.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/a442afec461d42b693fbda69339f3689.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9604b112-9c0b-5661-99bc-a927725a3aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164396} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43db8f-eb72-5379-ad90-7444b20614bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164397} {:answer/id #uuid "0028624f-4d5e-54f6-adb4-21cf50012c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164398} {:answer/id #uuid "7604128f-2e78-5d31-a4e5-14d621b093e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164399} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6f23f6-3aee-588d-a3b5-a87eb757b9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac43db8f-eb72-5379-ad90-7444b20614bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e9489f-6e99-5682-ae80-2e8eb0cd2e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164395}] :question/id #uuid "c356d0ae-4461-596b-a446-f5773fdb52cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-332920" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?"}]}
